          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %em Derived algebraic geometry
            is the specialization of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+algebraic+geometry"} homotopical algebraic geometry
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} (infinity,1)-category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+commutative+rings"} simplicial commutative rings
            (or sometimes, coconnective commutative
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-algebras"} dg-algebras
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
            where instead of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+rings"} commutative rings
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+schemes"} derived schemes
            are locally modelled on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+commutative+rings"} simplicial commutative rings
          %p
            Derived algebraic geometry is the correct setting for certain problems arising in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
            that involve
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersection+theory"} intersection theory
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+theory"} deformation theory
            (see below).
          %h2#terminology Terminology
          %p
            Sometimes the term
            %em derived algebraic geometry
            is also used for the related subject of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+algebraic+geometry"} spectral algebraic geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring+spectra"} commutative ring spectra
            are used instead of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+commutative+rings"} simplicial commutative rings
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+noncommutative+algebraic+geometry"} derived noncommutative algebraic geometry
          %h2#motivation Motivation
          %p There are several motivations for the study of derived algebraic geometry.
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hidden+smoothness+principle"} hidden smoothness principle
                of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"} Maxim Kontsevich
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
                %span.newWikiWord
                  smoothness
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/smoothness"} ?
                of certain
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+spaces"} moduli spaces
                is a consequence of the fact that they are in fact truncations of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+moduli+stacks"} derived moduli stacks
                (which are quasi-
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth"}> smooth
                ).
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersection+theory"}> Intersection theory
                \: a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric"} geometric
                interpretation of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Serre+intersection+formula"} Serre intersection formula
                for non-transverse
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersections"} intersections
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+theory"}> Deformation theory
                \: a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric"} geometric
                interpretation of the
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cotangent+complex"} cotangent complex
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝕃
                        %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{L}_X
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                makes sense as an object of derived algebraic geometry (a
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+stack"} derived stack
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cotangent+bundle"} cotangent bundle
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi T
                        %mo *
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^*X
                when
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                is smooth.
          %p
            For more detail on the final two points, see
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#VezzosiWhatIs"} (Vezzosi, 2011)
          %h2#history History
          %p
            The idea of developing a theory of schemes where structure sheaves are sheaves of dg-algebras was first proposed by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"} Maxim Kontsevich
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-schemes"} dg-schemes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ionut+Ciocan-Fontanine"} Ionut Ciocan-Fontanine
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mikhail+Kapranov"} Mikhail Kapranov
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+moduli+spaces"} derived moduli spaces
            (derived
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+scheme"} Hilbert scheme
            and derived
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quot+scheme"} Quot scheme
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+commutative+rings"} simplicial commutative rings
            in positive or mixed characteristic.
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bertrand+Toen"} Bertrand Toen
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gabriele+Vezzosi"} Gabriele Vezzosi
            developed
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+algebraic+geometry"} homotopical algebraic geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
            in any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HAG+context"} HAG context
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+model+category"} symmetric monoidal model category
            satisfying certain assumptions. As special cases they recover the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck"} Grothendieck
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+stacks"} higher stacks
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Carlos+Simpson"} Carlos Simpson
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+algebraic+geometry"} derived algebraic geometry
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complicial+algebraic+geometry"} complicial algebraic geometry
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                brave new algebraic geometry
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/brave+new+algebraic+geometry"} ?
          %p
            In his thesis
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"} Jacob Lurie
            also developed fundamentals of derived algebraic geometry, using the language of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structured+%28infinity%2C1%29-toposes"} structured (infinity,1)-toposes
            where
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bertrand+Toen"} Toen
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gabriele+Vezzosi"} Vezzosi
            used
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+toposes"} model toposes
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-%E2%88%9E+rings"} E-∞ rings
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+algebraic+geometry"} spectral algebraic geometry
          %h2#terminology_derived_versus_
            Terminology “derived” versus “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            \-”
          %p
            The adjective “derived” means pretty much the same as the “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            \-” in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-category"} ∞-category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functor"} derived functor
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %h3#structured_toposes
            structured
            = succeed "-toposes" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          %p
            In
            %a{:href => "#LurieStructured"} Lurie, Structured spaces
            a definition of
            %span.newWikiWord
              derived algebraic scheme
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/derived+algebraic+scheme"} ?
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+Deligne-Mumford+stack"} derived Deligne-Mumford stack
            is given in the wider context of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+scheme"} generalized scheme
            = succeed "es." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structured+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} structured (∞,1)-topos
          %p See these links for more details.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_derived_scheme_are_toposes
              Remark (derived scheme are
              = succeed "-toposes)" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %p
              This definition is based on the observation that it is a deficiency of the ordinary definition of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scheme"} scheme
              to demand that underlying a scheme is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
              and that a better definition is obtained by demanding it to have an underlying
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locale"} locale
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locale"} locale
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/0-topos"} 0-topos
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+scheme"} generalized scheme
              as a (locally affine,
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structured+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} structured
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
          %h2#RelationToDerivedNoncommutativeGeometry Relation to derived noncommutative geometry
          %p
            Under some conditions,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+schemes"} derived schemes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            in the sense of (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Structured+Spaces"}> Lurie, Structured Spaces
            ) are faithfully encoded by their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} stable (∞,1)-categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi QCoh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} QCoh(X)
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasicoherent+sheaves"} quasicoherent sheaves
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tannaka+duality+for+geometric+stacks"} Tannaka duality for geometric stacks
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quasi-Coherent+Sheaves+and+Tannaka+Duality+Theorems"} Lurie, Quasi-Coherent Sheaves and Tannaka Duality Theorems
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} stable (∞,1)-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            which is not of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi QCoh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} QCoh(X)
            for an actual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+scheme"} derived scheme
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            represents a generalized, “noncommutative” derived scheme. This is much like a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category+theory"} 2-category theory
            or rather
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C2%29-category"} (∞,2)-category
            theory analog of how in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
            the opposite of the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoids"} monoids
            (algebras) is regarded as a category of generalized spaces. This might be (and has been) called
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-algebraic+geometry"} 2-algebraic geometry
          %p
            Accordingly, one can decide to regard the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} opposite (∞,1)-category
            of suitable (e.g.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} monoidal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} stable (∞,1)-categories
            as being a category of “noncommutative derived schemes”. This is effectively the perspective on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+algebraic+geometry"} noncommutative algebraic geometry
            that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"} Maxim Kontsevich
            has been promoting.
          %p
            Often and traditionally, all this is expressed in terms of certain presentations for these
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} stable (∞,1)-categories
            by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+category"} triangulated
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+categories"} derived categories
            or better,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-enhancement"} enhancements
            as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-categories"} dg-categories
          %p
            In this fashion then in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+noncommutative+algebraic+geometry"} derived noncommutative algebraic geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            is by definition a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-category"} dg-category
            that is smooth and proper in an appropriate sense. The relation between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+algebraic+geometry"} noncommutative algebraic geometry
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+algebraic+geometry"} derived algebraic geometry
            may then be summed up by the adjunction
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Pf
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} DSt
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi op
                  %mo ⇄
                  %mi NCSp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi ℳ
                    %mo −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Pf : \DSt(k)^{op} \rightleftarrows NCSp(k) : \mathcal{M}_-
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Pf
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Pf(X)
            denotes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-category"} dg-category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perfect+complexes"} perfect complexes
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+stack"} derived stack
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℳ
                    %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}_\mathcal{C}
            denotes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+moduli+stack+of+objects+in+a+dg-category"} derived moduli stack of objects
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-category"} dg-category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+moduli+stack+of+objects+in+a+dg-category"} derived moduli stack of objects in a dg-category
            for details.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+algebraic+geometry"} homotopical algebraic geometry
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-%E2%88%9E+geometry"} E-∞ geometry
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-geometry"} dg-geometry
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+algebraic+geometry"} spectral algebraic geometry
            %li
              %span.newWikiWord
                brave new algebraic geometry
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/brave+new+algebraic+geometry"} ?
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+stacks"} higher stacks
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+differential+geometry"} higher differential geometry
            %li
